{**
 * c-offcanvas - a panel drawn in from an edge of the window over the page: a
 * basket, a set of filters, a menu on a narrow screen.
 *
 * It is the same native dialog c-modal is, shown as a modal and opened and
 * closed the same way - a button with commandfor naming it and the command
 * show-modal, and the cross in its header with the command close - so it
 * takes the focus in, makes the page behind it inert, closes on Escape and
 * gives the focus back, all of it the browser's. See modal.latte.
 *
 * What it adds is the edge (Trilobit\Core\Presentation\Component\OffcanvasEdge),
 * which is logical: the start and the end swap sides in a language written
 * from the right. It slides in from that edge, for nobody who asked for
 * reduced motion (assets/base.css).
 *
 * A click on the page beside it puts it away (closedby="any"), the way a menu
 * is put away: what it holds is something to look at or pick from rather than
 * something half written. A browser that does not know the attribute ignores
 * it, and there Escape and the cross still close it.
 *}

{import 'close.latte'}

{define offcanvas, string $id, string $title, Trilobit\Core\Presentation\Component\OffcanvasEdge $edge = Trilobit\Core\Presentation\Component\OffcanvasEdge::End, ?string $testId = null}
    <dialog
        n:class="'c-offcanvas', 'c-offcanvas--' . $edge->value"
        id="{$id}"
        aria-labelledby="{$id}-title"
        closedby="any"
        data-testid="{$testId}"
    >
        <div class="c-offcanvas__header">
            <h2 class="c-offcanvas__title" id="{$id}-title">{$title}</h2>

            {include close, attributes: ['commandfor' => $id, 'command' => 'close']}
        </div>

        <div class="c-offcanvas__body">{block offcanvasBody}{/block}</div>
    </dialog>
{/define}
